101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (The Credit Card Analytics project was undertaken to explore and analyse customer and transaction data, with the objective of converting raw business data into meaningful insights that can support better decision-making. The project focuses on understanding customer behaviour, analysing transaction patterns and monitoring important business performance indicators through interactive dashboards.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (I started by setting up a PostgreSQL database using pgAdmin 4, where I created a database and imported two CSV datasets into separate tables: Customer Data and Credit Card Transaction Data. Once the data was organised within the database, I established a connection between PostgreSQL and Microsoft Power BI using its PostgreSQL database connector to access the datasets for further analysis.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (After importing the data into Power BI, I used Power Query to perform the necessary data preparation and transformations. This included creating additional columns to support more detailed analysis and structuring the data for reporting purposes. I also developed DAX measures to calculate key performance indicators \(KPIs\) and enable dynamic analysis of customer and transaction performance.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (Based on the prepared data, I developed two separate interactive Power BI dashboards. The first dashboard focuses on credit card transaction analysis, providing an overview of transaction activity, spending patterns, financial performance and key trends. The second dashboard is dedicated to customer analytics, offering a closer look at customer characteristics, behaviour and other relevant performance metrics.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (The dashboards bring together important business metrics and visual insights in a structured format, making it easier to explore different aspects of credit card operations, identify trends and understand customer-related patterns. The overall approach was to make the data more accessible and useful rather than simply present numerical information.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (Through this project, I gained practical experience in creating and managing relational databases, importing external datasets, establishing database connectivity, transforming data using Power Query, developing DAX measures and building interactive business intelligence dashboards. The project demonstrates my ability to work through a complete data analytics workflow, from raw data integration to analytical reporting and business insight generation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (PROJECT OVERVIEW)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The Credit Card Financial Analytics project is a business intelligence and data analytics initiative focused on analysing credit card customer information and transaction records to gain a better understanding of financial performance, customer behaviour and transaction trends. The project aims to provide a consolidated view of credit card operations through interactive dashboards, making important business metrics easier to monitor, interpret and evaluate.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The project is built around two primary datasets: customer data and credit card transaction data. Both datasets were imported into a PostgreSQL database, which was created and managed using pgAdmin 4. This provided a structured environment for storing and accessing the underlying data. PostgreSQL was subsequently connected to Microsoft Power BI, allowing the datasets to be brought into the reporting environment for further processing and analysis.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The analytical work was divided into two complementary dashboards:)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (1. Credit Card Transaction Analysis: This dashboard focuses on the financial and operational aspects of credit card transactions. It provides an overview of revenue, transaction amounts, transaction activity, interest earned and other key performance indicators. It also helps examine weekly performance changes and identify trends across different transaction categories and time periods.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (2. Credit Card Customer Analysis: This dashboard focuses on customer-related information and segmentation. It provides insights into customer demographics, age groups, income categories, gender distribution and other relevant customer characteristics. The objective is to understand different customer segments and evaluate their contribution to overall business performance.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (Power Query was used to prepare and transform the imported datasets, including the creation of additional analytical columns. DAX was then used to develop calculated measures and key performance indicators required for the dashboards. These calculations enabled more detailed analysis of business metrics, including weekly comparisons and year-to-date performance.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The overall project follows a structured data analytics workflow, beginning with database creation and data integration, followed by data preparation, analytical calculations and interactive visualisation. By presenting customer and transaction information through two dedicated dashboards, the project demonstrates how business intelligence can help identify performance trends, understand customer segments and provide useful insights for business monitoring and decision-making.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
